{{also|föreställ}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|forestallen||to forestall, intercept, ambush, way-lay}}, from {{m|enm|forestalle||a forestalling, interception}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|foresteall||intervention, hindrance of justice, ambush}}, from {{m|ang|fore-||ahead of, before}} + {{m|ang|steall||position}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|fore|stall}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/fɔː(ɹ)ˈstɔːl/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɔːl|lang=en}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[prevent]], [[delay]] or [[hinder]] something by taking [[precautionary]] or [[anticipatory]] [[measure]]s; to [[avert]].
#: ''Fred '''forestalled''' disaster by his prompt action.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[preclude]] or [[bar]] from happening, render impossible.
#: ''In French, an aspired h '''forestalls''' [[elision]].''
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To purchase the complete supply of a good, particularly foodstuffs, in order to charge a [[monopoly]] price.
# To [[anticipate]], to act [[foreseeingly]].
#* Milton
#*: What need a man '''forestall''' his date of grief, / And run to meet what he would most avoid?
#* '''1919''', [[w:William Somerset Maugham|W. Somerset Maugham]], ''[[w:The Moon and Sixpence|The Moon and Sixpence]]'', [[s:The Moon and Sixpence/Chapter XXVI|chapter 26]]
#*: She insisted on doing her share of the offices needful to the sick. She arranged his bed so that it was possible to change the sheet without disturbing him. She washed him. {{...}} She did not speak to him much, but she was quick to '''forestall''' his wants.
# To [[deprive]] (with ''of'').
#* Shakespeare
#*: All the better; may / This night '''forestall''' him of the coming day!
# {{lb|en|UK|legal}} To obstruct or stop up, as a road; to stop the passage of a highway; to intercept on the road, as goods on the way to market.

